  "body": {
    "id": "prop:frontier-lower-envelope",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every admissible \\(k\\) and every \\(n\\geq4\\),\n\\[\n D_k(n)\\geq\n 2^{\\min\\{k+1,\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor\\}}.\n\\]\nThe lower bound is attained within the one-sink family by one original directed coefficient. At \\(n=5\\), the separate exact values are the sharper conclusions \\(D_1(5)=3\\) and \\(D_k(5)=4\\) for \\(k\\geq2\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "k",
      "D_kn"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:graph-class",
      "def:attaining-family",
      "def:attaining-membership-check",
      "thm:large-size-frontier",
      "thm:small-size-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Put\n\\[\n j=\\min\\{k,\\lfloor(n-4)/2\\rfloor\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(j\\leq k\\) and \\(n\\geq2j+4\\). Apply \\(\\mathrm{thm:large\\mbox{-}size\\mbox{-}frontier}\\) with excess budget \\(j\\). The one-sink graph \\(G^{\\star}_{j,n}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal G_{n,j}\\), and its original edge \\(e^{\\star}\\) satisfies\n\\[\n d_{e^{\\star}}(G^{\\star}_{j,n})=2^{j+1}.\n\\]\nBecause an input with at most \\(j\\) excess arrows also has at most \\(k\\) excess arrows, \\(\\mathrm{def:graph\\mbox{-}class}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal G_{n,j}\\subseteq\\mathcal G_{n,k}.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(D_k(n)\\geq2^{j+1}\\). For every integer \\(n\\geq4\\),\n\\[\n \\lfloor(n-4)/2\\rfloor+1=\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n j+1=\\min\\{k+1,\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor\\}.\n\\]\nSubstitution proves the displayed envelope. The final five-vertex values follow independently from \\(\\mathrm{thm:small\\mbox{-}size\\mbox{-}frontier}\\), and are stated separately because they exceed the one-sink envelope there.",
    "justification": "Class monotonicity turns the large-size one-sink construction into a certified lower bound at every size.",
    "gap": "The envelope need not be sharp in the remaining strip, as the sharper five-vertex cubic and quartic values demonstrate.",
    "consumer": "Any eventual all-size recurrence must dominate this explicit lower envelope.",
